 61    /** FUNCTION otap_logic.match_regex
 62    * Checks given data of type VARCHAR2 against an Oracle REGEX expression. Uses REGEXP_LIKE. As MATCHES is a reserved word
 63    * this is the equivalent of matches, imatches, doesnt_match and doesnt_imatch.
 64    * ATTENTION Oracle REGEX implementation is not standard. Unix regex which work like charm take hours to implement in
 65    * Oracle REGEX to work as desired. Test your expression well with Oracle before using it. Keep it simple to get it work.
 66    *
 67    * Easiest way to check is SELECT COUNT(*) FROM dual WHERE regexp_like('your string', 'your regex', 'regex param');
 68    * Should result in 1 if successful checked. You may want to prepare a with block with different string to pass them
 69    * through the regular expression.
 70    *
 71    * @param p_have The data to check.
 72    * @param p_regex A valid Oracle regular expression that p_have must match. Mandatory.
 73    * @param o_error Error information, if any, on the test executed.
 74    * @param p_param Optional valid parameter for REGEXP_LIKE. 'i' means case insensitive. Parameters are case sensitive. See Oracle documentation for details, https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/21/sqlrf/Pattern-matching-Conditions.html#GUID-D2124F3A-C6E4-4CCA-A40E-2FFCABFD8E19.
 75    * @param p_expected_result The expected test result as number. Default is test passed. See otap_constants.
 76    *
 77    * @return The test result as number, either otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_PASSED, otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_FAILED or otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_UNDEFINED.
 78    */
 79    FUNCTION match_regex( p_have	       IN	     VARCHAR2
 80  			   , p_regex	       IN	     VARCHAR2
 81  			   , o_errors		  OUT NOCOPY VARCHAR2
 82  			   , p_param	       IN	     VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
 83  			   , p_expected_result IN	     NUMBER   DEFAULT otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_PASSED
 84  			   )
 85  	 RETURN INTEGER
 86    ;
