416    /** FUNCTION otap_test.has_constraint
417    * Checks if a given table has a constraint of the given type. Optional you can specify column and constraint name.
418    *
419    * Supported constraint types:
420    * C - Check constraint on a table
421    * P - Primary key
422    * U - Unique key
423    * R - Referential integrity - use has_ref_constraint for more options
424    * V - With check option, on a view
425    * O - With read only, on a view
426    * H - Hash expression
427    * F - Constraint that involves a REF column - use has_ref_constraint for more options
428    * S is not supported, could not find or create an example to examine. SUPPLEMENTAL LOG clause only reflects in CDEF$ and CCOL$.
429    *
430    * @param p_table_name Mandatory. The table to be checked for constraint, take as is. Case sensitive. Missing value will cause test to fail.
431    * @param p_constraint_type Mandatory. A valid constraint type. Not case sensitive. Wrong values will cause the test to fail.
432    * @param p_column_name Optional. The column that is part of the constraint. Case sensitive.
433    * @param p_constraint Optional. The name of the constraint. Case sensitive.
434    * @param p_schema A schema override of the current test session if needed, taken as is. If given the table and constraint must exist in this schema. Case sensitive.
435    * @param p_description The test description if any. If not given, a description is generated, see template.
436    * @param p_expected_result The expected test result as number. Default is test passed. See otap_constants.
437    *
438    * @return The test result as text.
439    */
