808    /** FUNCTION otap_test.throws_ok
809    * Checks if a given statement or code block throws the expected error message. The expected error message must match
810    * the SQLERRM returned from exception thrown. Use throws_match or throws_like if only parts of the error message must
811    * match. If the statement starts with SELECT it is executed as is, without caring for returned columns or rows and ignoring
812    * any given header definition. The header definition is a code block for the declare section defining variables that may be
813    * needed by called functions or procedures. It is only used if the statement does not start with SELECT and if the declaration
814    * block can be executed with a NULL procedure without throwing exceptions.
815    *
816    * Function comes in two flavors, comparing SQLERRM as VARCHAR2 or the error code SQLCODE as number. It can also be used to check
817    * if a statement does not cause any exception by switching the expected result to otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_FAILED.
818    *
819    * SQL statements (SELECT, UPDATE, DELETE) MUST NOT have a trailing semicolon. otap will try to detect and remove it, but may fail.
820    * Semicolon in dynamically executed SQL statements will cause an unexpected exception.
821    *
822    * Functions and procedures MUST have a trailing semicolon, especially if more than one command is executed in the block.
823    *
824    * Syntax errors will most likely cause a different exception as the expected one and fail the test. This is especially important if
825    * you switch the expected test result. otap expects in this case, that the statement could be executed without any exception.
826    *
827    * @param p_statement Mandatory. The statement as string to execute. Can be a select statement or function/procedure call. No need to proovide a begin end block. Syntax should be checked or will cause unexpected exceptions.
828    * @param p_sqlerrm Mandatory. The exact case sensitive expected error message as returned by SQLERRM after a provoked exception.
829    * @param p_header_def Optional valid header definition (test result is undefined if header is not valid) for function or procedure tests to support OUT and return variables.
830    * @param p_description The test description if any. If not given, a description is generated, see template.
831    * @param p_expected_result The expected test result as number. Default is test passed. See otap_constants.
832    * @param p_schema A schema override of the current test session if needed, taken as is. Compares are not clearly associated to a schema. Case sensitive.
833    *
834    * @return The test result as text.
835    *
836    * Examples (english messages)
837    * SELECT otap_test.throws_ok('SELECT 1/0 FROM dual', 'ORA-01476: divisor is equal to zero') AS test_result FROM dual;
838    * SELECT otap_test.throws_ok('l_return := 1/0;', 'ORA-01476: divisor is equal to zero', 'l_return NUMBER;') AS test_result FROM dual;
839    */
840    FUNCTION throws_ok( p_statement	     IN VARCHAR2
841  			 , p_sqlerrm	     IN VARCHAR2
842  			 , p_header_def      IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
843  			 , p_description     IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
844  			 , p_expected_result IN NUMBER	 DEFAULT otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_PASSED
845  			 , p_schema	     IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT SYS_CONTEXT('USERENV', 'CURRENT_SCHEMA')
846  			 )
847  	 RETURN VARCHAR2
848    ;
849  
850    /** FUNCTION otap_test.throws_ok
851    * Flavor error code SQLCODE as number. Same behavior as flavor error message. Be aware that some Oracle error codes are group error codes where
852    * the error message differs depending on the exact error cause.
853    *
854    * @param p_statement Mandatory. The statement as string to execute. Can be a select statement or function/procedure call. No need to proovide a begin end block. Syntax should be checked or will cause unexpected exceptions.
855    * @param p_sqlcode Mandatory. The exact expected error code as returned by SQLCODE after a provoked exception.
856    * @param p_header_def Optional valid header definition (test result is undefined if header is not valid) for function or procedure tests to support OUT and return variables.
857    * @param p_description The test description if any. If not given, a description is generated, see template.
858    * @param p_expected_result The expected test result as number. Default is test passed. See otap_constants.
859    * @param p_schema A schema override of the current test session if needed, taken as is. Compares are not clearly associated to a schema. Case sensitive.
860    *
861    * @return The test result as text.
862    *
863    * Example
864    * SELECT otap_test.throws_ok('SELECT 1/0 FROM dual', -1476) AS test_result FROM dual;
865    */
866    FUNCTION throws_ok( p_statement	     IN VARCHAR2
867  			 , p_sqlcode	     IN NUMBER
868  			 , p_header_def      IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
869  			 , p_description     IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
870  			 , p_expected_result IN NUMBER	 DEFAULT otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_PASSED
871  			 , p_schema	     IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT SYS_CONTEXT('USERENV', 'CURRENT_SCHEMA')
872  			 )
873  	 RETURN VARCHAR2
874    ;
875  
876    /** FUNCTION otap_test.throws_matches
877    * Same as otap_test.throws_ok apart from using Oracle REGEXP to identify the error message.
878    *
879    * @param p_statement Mandatory. The statement as string to execute. Can be a select statement or function/procedure call. No need to proovide a begin end block. Syntax should be checked or will cause unexpected exceptions.
880    * @param p_regex_sqlerrm Mandatory. The regular expression to match the expected error message as returned by SQLERRM after a provoked exception.
881    * @param p_param Optional valid parameter for REGEXP_LIKE. 'i' means case insensitive. Parameters are case sensitive. See Oracle documentation for details, https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/21/sqlrf/Pattern-matching-Conditions.html#GUID-D2124F3A-C6E4-4CCA-A40E-2FFCABFD8E19.
882    * @param p_header_def Optional valid header definition (test result is undefined if header is not valid) for function or procedure tests to support OUT and return variables.
883    * @param p_description The test description if any. If not given, a description is generated, see template.
884    * @param p_expected_result The expected test result as number. Default is test passed. See otap_constants.
885    * @param p_schema A schema override of the current test session if needed, taken as is. Compares are not clearly associated to a schema. Case sensitive.
886    *
887    * @return The test result as text.
888    *
889    * Example (english message)
890    * SELECT otap_test.throws_matches('SELECT 1/0 FROM dual', '.*-01476: divisor is equal to zero.*') FROM dual;
891    */
892    FUNCTION throws_matches( p_statement	  IN VARCHAR2
893  			      , p_regex_sqlerrm   IN VARCHAR2
894  			      , p_param 	  IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
895  			      , p_header_def	  IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
896  			      , p_description	  IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
897  			      , p_expected_result IN NUMBER   DEFAULT otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_PASSED
898  			      , p_schema	  IN VARCHAR2 DEFAULT SYS_CONTEXT('USERENV', 'CURRENT_SCHEMA')
899  			      )
900  	 RETURN VARCHAR2
901    ;
902  
903    /** FUNCTION otap_test.throws_like
904    * Same as otap_test.throws_ok apart from using Oracle LIKE to identify the error message.
905    *
906    * @param p_statement Mandatory. The statement as string to execute. Can be a select statement or function/procedure call. No need to proovide a begin end block. Syntax should be checked or will cause unexpected exceptions.
